sront Business P,ofile: sAlL has presence across the steelvalue chain with an integrated crude steel manufacturint capacityof 20.84 MTPA as of March 2026. Ihe compaoy benefits from the presence of captive power plant and captive iron ore minesacross lharkhand, Odisha and Chhattisgarh, supportinE 100% of iron ore requirement. SAI['s 2%-3% of coal demand is metdomestically from its owned coal blocks and the balance through imports lrom Australia, the US, and New Zealand, and itsjointventure (jV)with Minas de Benga timitada {Mozambiqrre), a foreign Jv company or tnternationalcoalventures pvt. Ltd(a lV ofSAlL and others).
Furthermore, the company is undertaking various cost reduction initiatives through renewable power projects and expansionof its iron ore minint capacity under its modernisation and expansion (M&E) project, along with the development olcaptivecoking coal mine expansion over FY27-FY29. Furthermore, the business profile benefits from SAIL's wide marketing networkand diversified teographical presence. The management is focr,ssing on increasing the share of higher value-added products(FY26:56%; FY25t ss%) gradually over the near to medium term, leadang to an improvement in its sales revenue and supportsustained hieher profits.
lmprovin8 Techno Eaonomic PaEmeters: The company continues to improve on its techno-economic parameters, althoughit lags behind its lar8er peers- The improvement in techno economic parameters has been achieved usingefficient techniquessuch as coke dry quenching and coal dust injection, helpinB the company bring down the coke rare to 42ok&/ tonne of hotmetal (thm) in FY27 (FY26: 419k9/thm; tY25r 421k&/thm). The company continues to incur required maintenance capex fortechnology up8radation and upkeep of ats plants, while using raw material as per prescribed specifications to maantainoptimum specific energy consumption. Ihe company has increased the use of pellets in steel making to further improve theblast furnace productivity, which the management expects to improve further with the target of 1OO% of pellet utilisation inthe near to medium term sAlL has also been replacang the energy intensive ingot teeming method with the continuouscasting method.
Modetation in Workint Capital Requirement Resulting ln Reduction in Debt: SAtt's credit metrics improved in Fy26, led bya reduction in consolidated net adjusted debt (includinS lease liabilities and tc acceptances), on account of a decline ininventory and receivables, in line with lnd-Ra's expectation. SAIL's net debt reduced to tNR357 billion in Fy26 (Fy2S: lNR414billion; FY24: lNR465 billion). lts net adiusted leverage (net adjusted debt/operating EBTTDA) .educed to 2.97x in Fy26 (Fy25:3.89x; FYl4t 4.17xl, and the Sross interest coverage {operating EBITDA/eross interest expenses) increased to 8.4x (3.8r;4.5x).ln 1QFY27, the net debt remained around lNR358 billion with the net adjusted leverage of 2,Ox on annualised basis and theinterest coverage of 8.4x The inventory had substantially reduced owint to ag8ressive marketing, direct dispatches tocustomers and reduced debtors owing to collection from largest debtor railways where the collection was slow earlier.
lnd_Ra expects the net leverage to remain comfortable below 3.ox over FY27, supported by operating leverage from volumeramp-up from debottlenecking and increased capacitaes, reduced cost of production, higher share of value-idded productsalonS with better working capital management resuking in debt levels being maintained at current levels. However, theleverage could increase to 3.5x 4.0x during the peak capex phase over Fy2g-Fy3O.
Oebt-runded Capex: 5AlI plans to incur capex along with debottlenecking proiects over Fy27,Fy31. SAtL has commitmentstowards various capital_intensive projects for increasing capacity to 35 MMl by Fy31-Fy32 and expects a total outlay ofaround 1NR1,000 billion. The company also plans to enhance its existing capacity with processin8 and operating efficiencies.The majority of the capex will be undertaken over FY28-fY31 and will be funded through a debt of 55%-65% and the restthrough internal accruals. while the company has a track record of incurring a lower-than-expected/announced large capex,and witnessed delays in execution, this will remain a key rating monitorable. SAIL also has INR4O billion-So billion ofmaintenance capex liability every year which will be lunded through internal accruals.
Furthermore, the company will avail long-term debt to fund the planned brownfield capex Fy28 onwards. lnd-Ra willcontinue to monitor the terms of incremental capex debt and availability of moratorium and the resultant capital structure_Any additional stretch in its working capital cycle or higher than expected debt,funded capex without a commensurateincrease in cash flows will adversely affect credit ratios will remain a key rating monitorable.
Low but stable EBITOA/I; tikely to lmprove over FY27-FY28: SAIL'S EBITDA/I remained range-bound at around tNR6,000over FY24-FY25 and stood at 1NR6,030/t in FY26 (FY25:1NR5,948; FY24: INR 6,551) despite increased sales volumes- ThetBlT0A margins remained stable at 10.87o in FY26 (FY25: 10.4%; Fy24t 10.6%) accompanied by lower realisations. sAlL.sabsolute EBITDA increased 13% to tNR12O bi ion in Fy26 (Fy25: tNRlOG billion; Fy24: tNR111 balaon). tn 1eFy27, the EBTTDA/I

