lnd-Ra expects the EBITDA to remain stable around lNR8,sOO/t with the overall EBTTDA margintobe at t2%-14./o ovet Fy27-tY28, st pported by increased share of hiSher value-added products, operating efficiencie5 through modernisation initaativesundertaken by the company, and cost efficiency through increased backward integration in iron ore produation, reducedemployee cost and fuel cost and improved coke rate.
lndustry Risks:5Alt's cash flows remain exposed to the cyclicality inherent in the steel industry and the volatility in the pricesof raw materials, particularly coking coal. The company's inventory days also remain high, as it needs to maintain high rawmaterial inventory levels to ensure an adequate availability of imported inputs. Moreover, when the demand is subdued,SAlt's finished goods inventories also increase as production cuts cannot be implemented swaftly. However, SAlt has adiversified customer base, as it cater5 to various industries such as construction, infrastructure, capital goods and consumerdurables. With a further increase in the share of hiEhe. value-added products in defence and auto segment, the customerprofile would further diversify with hither rnargins industry. Also, most of it5 customers have stronE-to-adequate creditprofiles, thereby mitigating its counterparty collections risk to som€ extent.
Regulatory Risks: Given SAlt is involved in mining operations, it is exposed to th€ re8ulatory risk related to mining operations,mining lease and any contingent tax liabilities from the judgement of Supreme Court of lndaa's ruling on mineral rights andmineral bearing lands to the state governments. As of August 2026, the Gol has suggested for the removal of cess, whichwould benefit the company. However, any regulatory change would remain a key monitorable.
LiquidityAdequate: The averate maximum utilisation of the fund-based limits was around 65%, during the 12 months ended July2026. At end-June 2026, SAIL had adequate unutilised limits of around lNR37 billion from its working capital consortium andlNR381 billion from its short-term limits (outside the consortium). SAIL had unrestricted cash and equivalents of tNRo.96billion at end-March 2026 (FY25: lNR3.48 billion; fY24: NRo.69 billion). The net working capital cycle reduced to 76 days intY26 {FY25: 114 days; FY24: 130 days) and is likely to remain at similar levels in fY27, primarily due to a reduction in inventoryand receivable days. SAIL's cash flow from operations remained positive in FY26, and lnd,Ra expects the cash flow fromoperations to remain positive in FY27 due to sustained EBITDA and working capital release.
lnd'Ra opines SAIL's liquiditywill be sufficienl to meet its frrnd requirements over tY27-Fy28. With thescheduled repaymentsof lNR40.4 billion and lNR32.1 billion in FY27 and FY28, respectively. lnd-Ra expecrs SAtL's operational debt service coverageratio (DSCR) to be comfortable at 2x'3x over fY27-FY28. SAIL has high financial transparency and flexibility for access tocapital markets. The company has a long track record in the debt securities market, and its financing needs are adequatelymet through a mix of securities and diversified bank fundint. SAlL's forex risk arises largely from its overseas borrowingarrangements, which are primarily denominated in US dollar. The promoters did not have any shareholdjnS pledged as on30June 2025.
